    {
      "id": "b0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000112",
      "doc_id": "b0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "kind": "proof",
      "label": "Proof 3.4",
      "title": "Why Rank-Nullity holds",
      "statement_md": "Let T: V -> W be linear, with V finite dimensional. Choose a basis v(1), ..., v(k) of the kernel of T. By Basis extension, complete this to a basis v(1), ..., v(n) of V. We claim T(v(k+1)), ..., T(v(n)) is a basis of the image of T. Linearity shows that these vectors span the image. To prove independence, suppose a linear combination of them is zero. Then the corresponding combination of v(k+1), ..., v(n) lies in ker T. By Kernel and image, this is a combination of v(1), ..., v(k). Independence of the full basis forces every coefficient to vanish. Thus dim ker T = k, while dim im T = n - k. Adding the two dimensions gives dim V = n, as required.",
      "clauses": [],
      "symbols": [],
